'''''Polyscias''''' is a genus of [[flowering plant]]s in the family [[Araliaceae]] with a natural distribution from tropical Africa through Southeast Asia and Australia to islands of the Pacific. {{As of|2026|2}}, [[Plants of the World Online]] recognises 28 synonyms and about 180 species for the genus.{{R|POWO}}

==Description==
They are shrubs, trees or (rarely) lianes, which are mostly [[evergreen]] or occasionally [[deciduous]]. The leaves are alternate and have a membraneous or winged sheathing base of the [[petiole (botany)|petiole]]s. [[Stipule]]s are either absent or vestigial. Leaf morphology is varied: they may be undivided or [[Pinnation#Number_of_divisions|pinnate]], bipinnate or tripinnate, and the margins may be [[Glossary of leaf morphology#entire|entire]], toothed or lobed.{{R|KUBITZKI|LOWRY}}

Flower characters are likewise varied, and inflorescences may be erect or pendant, and take the form of [[panicle]]s, [[umbel]]s, [[raceme]]s, or whorls.

==Taxonomy==
The genus was erected in 1776 by [[Johann Reinhold Forster]] and his son [[Georg Forster]], to accommodate a plant specimen they had collected in the South Pacific.{{R|LOWRY|FORSTER}}

===Etymology===
The name ''Polyscias'' is derived from the Greek words [[wikt:πολύς|πολύς]] (''polys''), 'many', and ''skias'', 'shade' or 'umbrella', and is a reference to the foliage of the plants.{{R|NCU}}

==Cultivation==
There are about six species of the [[genus]] ''Polyscias'' that are actively cultivated. The genus contains a variety of tropical plants, which include about 80 species from the Pacific islands and Southeast Asia.{{R|NCU}}

